---
title: Basic Roasted Duck
prepTime: 15m
cookTime: 4h15m
description: "The best duck recipe, glaze or no glaze"
tags: Duck
---

Preheat the oven to 300°F.

Remove the neck, gizzards, and anything else from the cavity of the @duck{1}.
Rinse the bird and pat dry.

Place the duck in a roasting pan on a wire rack to keep the meat and skin out of the juices and to allow air flow.
Score the skin, cut off excess fat, and poke it all over.

> Stretch out any of the excess removed skin, sprinkle with skin, and place it on the wire rack during roasting.
> Remove the skin after the second or third hour after it has crisped up to almost brittle.
> The result will be a super crispy duck skin with loads of flavor to snack on while the duck finishes cooking.

@Salt the inside and outside of the duck, and truss the legs with cooking twine.

Roast for ~{1%hour}, breast-side up.

Poke, flip, and pour out any accumulated juices from the bird's cavity into the pan. Roast for ~{1%hour}, breast-side down.

Poke, flip, and pour out any accumulated juices from the bird's cavity into the pan. Roast for ~{1%hour}, breast-side up.

Poke, flip, and pour out any accumulated juices from the bird's cavity into the pan. Roast for ~{1%hour}, breast-side down.
Depending on the size of your duck and how done it is before this step, the time may be decreased so as to not overcook the meat.
The meat at the thickest part should register 165°F at the end of this step.

Poke, flip, breast-side up.
Increase the temperature to 400°F and cook for ~{10%minutes}.

Brush with @glaze{}(optional) if using.
Finish at 400°F for ~{5-7%minutes}, taking care not to burn the sauce.

Rest, carve, and serve.
